beat:
In speaking, which confused the sense of much;
Her heart, against his words, beat out so thick,
They might as well be written on the dust

beard:
He lifted the pail, new milk he quaffed;
Then wiping his curly black beard like silk:
'Whitest cow that ever was calved

beam:
She hangs like a star in the dew of our song;
She springs like a beam on the brow of the tide,
She falls like a tear from the eyes of a bride.

bear:
We say ‘my God’ sometimes, upon our knees,
Who is not therefore vexed: so bear with it ...
And me. I know I’m foolish, weak, and vain;

beak:
The thunder hurried slow;
The lightning showed a yellow beak,
And then a livid claw.

beats:
When it does the will of an honest mind;
When it beats with love fur all mankind;
Then the night but leads to a fairer day--

beaches:
Fruits red and purple, somber-bloomed and black;
Then, down rich fields and frosty river beaches
We'll trample bright persimmons, while we kill

beauties:
At close of day no longer walks the sky;
Pity me not for beauties passed away
From field and thicket as the year goes by;

Or claim the Muses with the Mantuan Sage;
Soon the same beauties should my mind adorn,
And the same ardors in my soul should burn:

beaming:
There sits, illustrious Sir, thy beauteous spouse;
A gem-blaz'd circle beaming on her brows.
Hail'd with acclaim among the heav'nly choirs,

beamed:
To shrivel with crackling from before my face;
Across mine eyes a waxing radiance beamed
And showed a certain place.

beau:
Sallie dear;
How she say dat I 's huh beau,
An' hit tickles me to know

beaux:
A town of gaiety and sport,
Where beaux and beauteous nymphs resort,
And gallantry doth reign;

beacon:
Croaking, croaking, croaking:--
Let the beacon-fire blaze higher;
Bridegroom, watch; the Bride draws nigher.

beauty's:
Poor withered rose, thou liest dead;
Too soon thy beauty's bloom hath fled.
'Tis not without a tearful ruth

bearin:
She was de preachah's favoured, an' he tol' de chu'ch one night
Dat she travelled thoo de cloud o' sin a-bearin' of a light;
But, now, I 'low he t'inkin' dat she mus' 'a' los' huh lamp,

beamin:
He jest turned roun' an' smacked us all, an' kep' right on a-drivin'.
Well, soon the schoolhouse hove in sight, the winders beamin' brightly;
The sound o' talkin' reached our ears, and voices laffin' lightly.

beatenes:
Dat ol' mare o' mine;
She de beatenes' t'ing dat evah struck de medders o' de town,
An' aldough huh haid ain't fittin' fu' to waih no golden crown,

beautify:
To crown her dear old head;
And to beautify the pathway
Where winter still did tread.

I'd buy a little scrubbing-brush
And beautify the floors;
I'd warble gaily as a thrush

beauteous:
Where heav'n's Supreme in bliss and glory reigns.
There sits, illustrious Sir, thy beauteous spouse;
A gem-blaz'd circle beaming on her brows.

